        'example'   => '<p>0.5 coin bought at $40,000, sold at $65,000, 0.1% fee:</p><ul><li>Cost: <strong>$20,020</strong>; proceeds: <strong>$32,467.50</strong></li><li>Net profit: <strong>$12,447.50</strong>; ROI <strong>62.18%</strong></li><li>Fees paid: <strong>$52.50</strong>; break-even price <strong>$40,080.08</strong></li></ul>',
        'sections'  => [
            [ 'Fees change the break-even point', '<p>Because you pay a fee when buying and again when selling, the price must rise slightly above your purchase price before you make money. The break-even price shows exactly how far.</p>' ],
            [ 'Tax on crypto gains', '<p>The IRS treats crypto as property. Gains on assets held more than a year are generally taxed at the lower long-term capital gains rates; those held a year or less are taxed as ordinary income. Use the capital gains tax calculator to estimate the tax.</p>' ],
            [ 'What this does not include', '<p>Network (gas) fees, withdrawal fees, spread and taxes are not included unless you add them into the fee percentage. Keep records of every trade for your tax return.</p>' ],
            [ 'Why fees matter more than most traders expect', '<p>A 0.1% fee on each side of a trade sounds negligible, but it compounds with trade frequency: someone making dozens of trades a month on a high-fee exchange can lose a meaningful share of their gains to fees alone, even before accounting for taxes. Comparing the fee schedules of different exchanges is worth doing before you start trading actively, not after.</p>' ],
            [ 'Dollar-cost averaging into crypto changes this math', '<p>If you bought at several different prices over time rather than in one lump sum, your real "cost basis" is a weighted average across all those purchases, not any single buy price. Calculate your profit against that blended average cost, not your most recent or most memorable purchase price, for an accurate picture.</p>' ],
            [ 'Short-term vs. long-term capital gains — why the holding period matters', '<p>In the U.S., crypto held for one year or less before selling is taxed as ordinary income, which can mean a noticeably higher rate than the preferential long-term capital gains rates that apply after holding more than a year. This single date — whether you\'re a day over or a day under the one-year mark — can change your actual tax bill substantially on a large gain.</p>' ],
