        'example'   => '<p>$10,000 at 12%, monthly compounding, 12 months:</p><ul><li>Final value: <strong>$11,268.25</strong></li><li>Rewards: <strong>$1,268.25</strong> (about <strong>$105.69</strong> a month)</li><li>Effective APY: <strong>12.68%</strong></li></ul>',
        'sections'  => [
            [ 'APR vs APY', '<p>APR is the stated yearly rate without compounding. APY includes compounding, so it is higher when rewards compound more often. Enter the stated rate and choose the compounding frequency to see the APY.</p>' ],
            [ 'The calculator assumes a fixed rate', '<p>Real staking yields change with the network and how many people stake. Rewards are usually paid in the coin itself, so their dollar value moves with the price. Lock-up periods and slashing penalties can also apply.</p>' ],
            [ 'Taxes', '<p>Under IRS Revenue Ruling 2023-14, a cash-method taxpayer who stakes on a proof-of-stake network must include staking rewards in gross income in the year they gain dominion and control over them, at fair market value on that date. The IRS says to report this income on Schedule 1 of Form 1040. Selling the rewards later can create a capital gain or loss. Keep records and see the IRS digital-assets guidance or a tax professional.</p>' ],
            [ 'Why advertised staking yields can be deceptive', '<p>A pool or exchange advertising a high fixed APY often funds part of that yield from its own token emissions, which dilutes the token\'s price as more gets minted — meaning the dollar value of your stake can still fall even while the coin count visibly grows. A high headline APY is not automatically a high real return once price dilution is factored in.</p>' ],
            [ 'Lock-up periods and liquidity risk', '<p>Many staking programs require locking your coins for a set period, during which you cannot sell or move them even if the price drops sharply or you need the cash. Some networks also impose an "unbonding period" after you request to unstake, often days to weeks, before your coins actually become available. Factor this illiquidity into your decision, not just the advertised yield.</p>' ],
            [ 'Typical crypto staking APY by asset (November 2026)', '<p>Reward rates below are network-wide figures from StakingRewards.com and the Lido API, checked in November 2026. They are variable and not guaranteed, and they change daily. Verify the current rate before you stake.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Asset</th><th>Reward rate</th><th>Minimum stake</th><th>Unstaking period</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ETH, solo/home staking</td><td>about 2.5%</td><td>32 ETH per validator (ethereum.org)</td><td>Exit and withdrawal queue; can stretch to weeks when many validators exit</td></tr><tr><td>ETH via Lido (stETH)</td><td>about 2.3% after Lido’s 10% fee on rewards</td><td>Any amount</td><td>Sell stETH on the market at any time, or queue a withdrawal through the protocol</td></tr><tr><td>Solana (SOL)</td><td>about 4.9%</td><td>No protocol minimum to delegate</td><td>About one epoch (around 2–3 days)</td></tr><tr><td>Cosmos Hub (ATOM)</td><td>about 19.7%, but inflation is about 12.7%</td><td>No meaningful minimum</td><td>21 days</td></tr><tr><td>Cardano (ADA)</td><td>about 2.1%</td><td>No protocol minimum; a refundable 2 ADA deposit registers a stake address</td><td>None; ADA stays liquid</td></tr><tr><td>Polkadot (DOT)</td><td>about 2.8%</td><td>1 DOT through a nomination pool</td><td>28 days (a proposed change could shorten it when the queue is idle)</td></tr></tbody></table>' ],
            [ 'Native vs. exchange vs. liquid staking', '<ul><li><strong>Native (solo or delegated) staking:</strong> you keep custody of your coins and pay only the validator’s commission. You take on unbonding delays and, on some networks, slashing risk. Solo ETH staking also requires 32 ETH and running hardware.</li><li><strong>Exchange staking:</strong> simple, with no minimum, but the exchange holds your coins (custodial and counterparty risk) and keeps a share of the rewards as a commission. Exchanges publish their commission rates in their staking terms, so check the current figure. It can take a large share of a low base yield.</li><li><strong>Liquid staking (for example Lido stETH):</strong> you receive a token you can sell or use elsewhere, so liquidity is better. Lido takes 10% of rewards. You add smart-contract risk, and the token can trade below the value of the underlying ETH during market stress.</li></ul><p>No option is right for everyone. Weigh custody, fees, liquidity and technical risk against your own needs.</p>' ],
            [ 'Net APY after fees and gas', '<p>On networks where you must claim and restake rewards yourself, each transaction costs a fee. Here is an illustrative example (gas costs vary widely, from cents to several dollars or more when the network is busy):</p><ul><li>$1,000 staked at 4% APY earns about <strong>$40 a year</strong>.</li><li>Claiming and restaking monthly at an assumed $5 per round costs <strong>$60 a year</strong>, a net loss of about $20.</li><li>Compounding once a year instead costs about $5, leaving roughly $35.</li></ul><p>Monthly instead of annual compounding adds only about 0.07 percentage points at 4%, so for small balances, compound less often. Many liquid-staking tokens and exchanges compound for you with no per-claim gas.</p>' ],
            [ 'Real yield = APY minus token inflation', '<p>New staking rewards are usually created by issuing new tokens, which dilutes every holder. A rough measure of your real yield is the reward rate minus the inflation rate. For example, in November 2026 Cosmos Hub paid about 19.7% while ATOM supply inflation was about 12.7%. That leaves a real yield of roughly 7%, or about 6.2% measured precisely as (1.197 ÷ 1.127) − 1. Holders who do not stake are diluted by the full 12.7%. Price moves can outweigh both numbers.</p><p><strong>APR to APY</strong> (APY = (1 + APR ÷ n)<sup>n</sup> − 1):</p><table><thead><tr><th>APR</th><th>Annual</th><th>Monthly</th><th>Daily</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5%</td><td>5.00%</td><td>5.12%</td><td>5.13%</td></tr><tr><td>20%</td><td>20.00%</td><td>21.94%</td><td>22.13%</td></tr></tbody></table>' ],
            [ 'Not investment advice', '<p>This calculator and page are for education only and are not investment, tax or legal advice. Every rate shown is variable, not guaranteed, and based on data from a single point in time. Crypto prices are volatile, and you can lose money even while earning rewards.</p>' ],
            [ 'Validator selection and slashing risk', '<p>On many proof-of-stake networks, you (or the pool you delegate to) choose a specific validator to stake with, and that validator\'s behavior affects your rewards. A poorly run or malicious validator can be "slashed" — penalized by the network — which can reduce or eliminate the staked funds delegated to it. Researching a validator\'s uptime history and reputation matters just as much as the advertised yield.</p>' ],
        ],
        'terms'     => [ [ 'Staking', 'Locking coins to help run a proof-of-stake network in return for rewards.' ], [ 'APY', 'Annual percentage yield, including compounding.' ], [ 'Slashing', 'A penalty on staked coins for validator misbehavior or downtime.' ], [ 'Unbonding period', 'A mandatory waiting period after requesting to unstake, before your coins become available to move or sell.' ] ],
        'faqs'      => [
            [ 'How do you calculate staking rewards?', 'Grow the staked amount by the rate each compounding period: final value = amount × (1 + APR ÷ n)^(n × years). For example, $10,000 at 12% compounded monthly for a year becomes $11,268.25, which is $1,268.25 in rewards. Rewards are usually paid in the coin, so their dollar value depends on its price.' ],
            [ 'What is a good staking APY?', 'Yields vary by coin and change constantly. In November 2026, major networks ranged from about 2–3% (ETH, ADA, DOT) to about 5% (SOL) to nearly 20% (ATOM). A high APY is not automatically better: compare it with the token’s inflation rate and risk.' ],
            [ 'Are staking rewards taxable?', 'In the U.S., yes. Revenue Ruling 2023-14 says a cash-method taxpayer includes staking rewards in gross income when they gain dominion and control over them, at fair market value at that time. A later sale can also trigger capital gains tax.' ],
            [ 'Can I lose money staking?', 'Yes. A 5% reward does not offset a 30% price drop. Slashing can cut staked funds on some networks, and unbonding periods (21 days on ATOM, 28 on DOT) can stop you selling during a crash.' ],
            [ 'Which crypto has the highest staking APY?', 'Among large networks, Cosmos Hub (ATOM) showed one of the higher rates in November 2026 at about 19.7%. Much of that comes from about 12.7% token inflation. Smaller networks may advertise even more, usually with higher risk. The highest headline APY is rarely the highest real return.' ],
            [ 'Do staking rewards compound automatically?', 'It depends on the network and platform. Lido’s stETH balance grows daily without any action, and Polkadot nomination pools can restake for you. On other setups you must claim and restake yourself, often paying a transaction fee each time.' ],
            [ 'How much can I earn staking $1,000 of ETH?', 'At the roughly 2.5% network rate seen in November 2026, about $25 a year, or about $23 through Lido after its 10% fee. Rewards are paid in ETH, so the dollar value moves with the price. Rates are variable, not guaranteed.' ],
            [ 'Is staking safe?', 'No form of staking is risk-free. Risks include price volatility, slashing, lock-ups, smart-contract bugs on liquid staking, and custody or platform failure on exchanges. Spread risk, read the terms, and stake only what you can afford to have locked or lose.' ],
            [ 'Is this the same as a crypto APY or crypto interest calculator?', 'Yes. Staking rewards calculator, crypto APY calculator and crypto interest calculator all describe the same math: growing a staked or deposited amount by a yield rate over time, with optional compounding. This tool works whether your yield comes from staking, an exchange savings product, or a DeFi lending platform.' ],
            [ 'Why might a very high advertised staking APY be a red flag?', 'Extremely high yields are often funded by the protocol minting new tokens, which dilutes the token supply and can push the coin\'s dollar price down even as your coin count grows — so the real, dollar-denominated return can be much lower than the advertised percentage.' ],
            [ 'What happens if I want to unstake early?', 'Many networks impose a lock-up or "unbonding" period before your coins become available again, sometimes days or weeks. Confirm the specific terms for your network or platform before staking funds you may need access to soon.' ],
        ],
        'sources'   => [ [ 'IRS: Digital assets', 'https://www.irs.gov/filing/digital-assets' ], [ 'IRS Revenue Ruling 2023-14', 'https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rr-23-14.pdf' ], [ 'StakingRewards.com: asset reward rates (Nov 2026)', 'https://www.stakingrewards.com/' ], [ 'Lido docs: protocol fee and APR', 'https://docs.lido.fi/' ], [ 'ethereum.org: staking', 'https://ethereum.org/en/staking/' ], [ 'Polkadot Wiki: staking', 'https://wiki.polkadot.network/docs/learn-staking' ] ],
        'related'   => [ 'crypto-pl-calculator', 'crypto-converter', 'compound-interest-calculator', 'mining-profitability-calculator' ],
    ],

    'mining-profitability-calculator' => [
        'focus'     => 'bitcoin mining profitability calculator',
        'secondary' => [ 'bitcoin mining calculator', 'mining profit calculator', 'asic mining calculator', 'bitcoin mining break even' ],
        'title'     => 'Bitcoin Mining Profitability Calculator',
        'meta'      => 'Free Bitcoin mining calculator: enter hash rate, power, electricity cost, network hash rate and block reward to see daily profit and the break-even price.',
        'excerpt'   => 'Estimate daily and monthly Bitcoin mining profit after electricity and pool fees.',
        'answer'    => 'A 110 TH/s miner using 3,250 W at $0.12 per kWh on a 900 EH/s network with a 3.125 BTC block reward mines about 0.0000545 BTC a day. At $80,000 per bitcoin that is about $4.36 of revenue against $9.36 of electricity, a loss of about $5.00 a day. The break-even price is about $171,901.',
        'steps'     => [ 'Enter your miner’s hash rate and power use.', 'Enter your electricity price per kWh.', 'Enter the network hash rate (from mempool.space), the Bitcoin price and the block reward.', 'Enter the pool fee and read the daily profit, monthly profit and break-even price.' ],
        'formula'   => '<p><strong>BTC per day = your hash rate ÷ network hash rate × 144 blocks × block reward × (1 − pool fee).</strong></p><p><strong>Revenue = BTC per day × price. Electricity per day = kW × 24 × price per kWh. Profit = revenue − electricity.</strong> Break-even price = electricity ÷ BTC per day.</p>',
        'example'   => '<p>110 TH/s, 3,250 W, $0.12/kWh, 900 EH/s network, 3.125 BTC reward, 1% fee, $80,000 BTC:</p><ul><li>Mined: <strong>0.00005445 BTC</strong> a day</li><li>Revenue: <strong>$4.36</strong>; electricity: <strong>$9.36</strong></li><li>Profit: <strong>−$5.00</strong> a day (about <strong>−$150</strong> a month)</li><li>Break-even price: <strong>$171,901</strong></li></ul>',
        'sections'  => [
            [ 'Electricity decides profitability', '<p>The biggest cost is power. At $0.12 per kWh an older, less efficient machine often loses money, while cheap electricity and efficient hardware can still earn a profit. Compare machines by efficiency in joules per terahash.</p>' ],
            [ 'What changes over time', '<p>Network hash rate and difficulty rise as more miners join, which cuts your share of rewards. The block reward halves roughly every four years. Bitcoin’s price moves constantly. Update the inputs regularly.</p>' ],
            [ 'What is not included', '<p>Hardware cost, cooling, hosting, repairs and transaction fees included in blocks are not in this estimate. Mined coins are taxable income in the U.S. at their value when received, and later sales may add capital gains.</p>' ],
            [ 'Compare machines by efficiency, not just hash rate', '<p>Efficiency is power divided by hash rate, in joules per terahash (J/TH). The example machine uses 3,250 W for 110 TH/s, or about 29.5 J/TH. A newer 200 TH/s machine drawing 3,500 W runs at 17.5 J/TH. With the same network (900 EH/s), reward (3.125 BTC), 1% pool fee and $80,000 bitcoin:</p><ul><li><strong>Older 110 TH/s machine at $0.12/kWh:</strong> $4.36 revenue, $9.36 power, <strong>−$5.00 a day</strong>.</li><li><strong>Newer 200 TH/s machine at $0.12/kWh:</strong> $7.92 revenue, $10.08 power, <strong>−$2.16 a day</strong>.</li><li><strong>Newer machine at $0.06/kWh:</strong> $7.92 revenue, $5.04 power, <strong>+$2.88 a day</strong>, about $86 a month.</li></ul><p>Hardware efficiency narrows the gap, but the electricity rate usually decides whether a machine makes money at all.</p>' ],
            [ 'Your break-even electricity price', '<p>Divide daily revenue by the kilowatt-hours used per day. The 110 TH/s machine uses 78 kWh a day (3.25 kW × 24), so at $4.36 of revenue it breaks even at about <strong>$0.056 per kWh</strong>. The 200 TH/s machine uses 84 kWh and breaks even at about <strong>$0.094 per kWh</strong>. Typical U.S. household rates are well above both figures, which is why most profitable mining runs on industrial or off-peak power. Remember that a break-even rate covers electricity only, not the cost of the machine.</p>' ],
            [ 'Payback period and the next halving', '<p>Profit has to repay the hardware before it is real profit. If the newer machine costs $4,000 and earns $2.88 a day, payback takes about 1,389 days, or roughly 3.8 years, assuming nothing changes. Something will: the block reward is expected to halve to 1.5625 BTC around 2028. With every other input held the same, that cuts the machine’s revenue to $3.96 a day against $5.04 of power, a loss of $1.08 a day. Network hash rate also tends to rise over time, shrinking your share further. Test your plan with a lower price, a higher network hash rate and a halved reward before buying hardware.</p>' ],
        ],
        'terms'     => [ [ 'Hash rate', 'How many calculations per second a miner performs (TH/s = trillion, EH/s = quintillion).' ], [ 'Block reward', 'New bitcoin awarded for each block mined, 3.125 BTC after the April 2024 halving.' ], [ 'Pool fee', 'The share of rewards a mining pool keeps.' ] ],
        'faqs'      => [
            [ 'Is Bitcoin mining profitable?', 'It depends on your electricity cost, hardware efficiency and the Bitcoin price. At typical household electricity prices many machines lose money.' ],
            [ 'How is mining profit calculated?', 'Multiply your share of the network by the daily block rewards, convert to dollars, and subtract electricity and pool fees.' ],
            [ 'Where do I find the network hash rate?', 'Public sites such as mempool.space show the current figure. Enter it in EH/s.' ],
            [ 'Is mined bitcoin taxable?', 'Yes. In the U.S. it is income at the fair market value when you receive it.' ],
            [ 'What electricity price makes Bitcoin mining profitable?', 'It depends on the machine. With bitcoin at $80,000 and a 900 EH/s network, an older 29.5 J/TH machine breaks even at about $0.056 per kWh and a newer 17.5 J/TH machine at about $0.094 per kWh, before counting hardware costs.' ],
            [ 'How does the Bitcoin halving affect mining profit?', 'A halving cuts the block reward in half, so revenue per terahash roughly halves overnight while electricity costs stay the same. A machine earning $2.88 a day at 3.125 BTC would lose about $1.08 a day at 1.5625 BTC if the price and network hash rate did not change.' ],
        ],
        'sources'   => [ [ 'IRS: Digital assets', 'https://www.irs.gov/filing/digital-assets' ] ],
        'related'   => [ 'crypto-pl-calculator', 'crypto-converter', 'break-even-calculator', 'staking-rewards-calculator' ],
    ],

    'va-loan-calculator' => [
        'focus'     => 'va loan calculator',
        'secondary' => [ 'va mortgage calculator', 'va home loan calculator', 'va loan payment calculator', 'va loan vs conventional' ],
        'title'     => 'VA Loan Calculator: Payment, Funding Fee and Entitlement',
        'meta'      => 'Free VA loan calculator: monthly payment, VA funding fee by use and down payment, zero down and no PMI, and a full comparison to a conventional loan today.',
        'excerpt'   => 'Estimate your VA home loan payment, including the funding fee, and compare it with a conventional loan.',
        'answer'    => 'On a $400,000 home with no down payment, a first-use VA loan has a 2.15% funding fee of $8,600. Financed, the loan becomes $408,600, and at 6.5% over 30 years the principal-and-interest payment is about $2,582.63 a month, compared with $2,528.27 without the fee. There is no monthly mortgage insurance on a VA loan.',
        'steps'     => [ 'Enter the home price and your down payment.', 'Choose the loan type, your military category and whether you have used your benefit before.', 'Enter the interest rate and loan term.', 'Read the funding fee, total loan amount and monthly payment, and compare with a conventional loan.' ],
        'formula'   => '<p><strong>Funding fee = loan amount × fee rate</strong> (it can be financed). Monthly principal and interest = <strong>P × r ÷ (1 − (1 + r)<sup>−n</sup>)</strong> where P is the loan including the financed fee, r the monthly rate and n the number of payments.</p>',
        'example'   => '<p>$400,000 home, 0% down, first use, 2.15% fee, 6.5%, 30 years:</p><ul><li>Funding fee: <strong>$8,600</strong>; loan: <strong>$408,600</strong></li><li>Principal and interest: <strong>$2,582.63</strong> a month (<strong>$2,528.27</strong> without the fee)</li></ul><p>Taxes, insurance and HOA are added on top. The 6.5% rate is an illustration.</p>',
        'sections'  => [
            [ 'How This Calculator Works', '<figure style="margin:20px 0;text-align:center"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 160" width="100%" style="max-width:600px;height:auto;font-family:-apple-system,Segoe UI,Roboto,sans-serif"><title>How the VA loan calculator works</title><desc>Enter the home price and your military category. The calculator applies the funding fee automatically. Compare the result against a conventional loan.</desc><rect x="0" y="0" width="680" height="160" rx="20" fill="#F8FAFC"/><rect x="24" y="40" width="190" height="80" rx="14" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#00C896" stroke-width="2"/><text x="119" y="72" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="800" fill="#0F172A">1. Enter details</text><text x="119" y="94" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="#64748B">price, category, use</text><path d="M220 80 L250 80" stroke="#94A3B8" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#vaArrow1)"/><rect x="260" y="40" width="190" height="80" rx="14" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#3B82F6" stroke-width="2"/><text x="355" y="72" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="800" fill="#0F172A">2. See funding fee</text><text x="355" y="94" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="#64748B">applied automatically</text><path d="M456 80 L486 80" stroke="#94A3B8" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#vaArrow2)"/><rect x="496" y="40" width="160" height="80" rx="14" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#0F172A" stroke-width="2"/><text x="576" y="72" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="800" fill="#0F172A">3. Compare</text><text x="576" y="94" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="#64748B">vs conventional</text><defs><marker id="vaArrow1" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="6" refY="4" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z" fill="#94A3B8"/></marker><marker id="vaArrow2" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="6" refY="4" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z" fill="#94A3B8"/></marker></defs></svg></figure><p>Enter the home price, your military category and whether this is a first or subsequent use above. The calculator applies the correct funding fee automatically and shows your monthly payment side by side with a conventional loan.</p>' ],
            [ 'Why VA loans are popular', '<p>Eligible veterans, service members and some surviving spouses can buy with no down payment, no monthly mortgage insurance and competitive rates. Instead there is a one-time funding fee unless you are exempt.</p>' ],
            [ 'VA vs conventional', '<p>A conventional loan with under 20% down usually requires private mortgage insurance. A VA loan replaces it with the funding fee, which is often cheaper over the life of the loan. Compare both in the calculator for your numbers.</p>' ],
            [ 'Eligibility', '<p>You need a Certificate of Eligibility based on your service. The VA explains requirements, entitlement and limits on its home-loan pages.</p>' ],
            [ 'VA loan limits and entitlement explained', '<p>Unlike FHA loans, VA loans have no official maximum loan amount set by the VA itself for borrowers with full entitlement — lenders instead look at your income and debt-to-income ratio to decide what you qualify for. Borrowers with reduced entitlement, for example because an earlier VA loan is still active, may face county-specific limits that affect how large a loan they can get with zero down.</p>' ],
            [ 'Can you use a VA loan more than once?', '<p>Yes. VA loan benefits can be reused, including simultaneously for a second property in some cases if enough entitlement remains from the first loan. Subsequent use carries a higher funding fee than a first-time use with the same down payment, which the calculator reflects when you select "subsequent use."</p>' ],
            [ 'VA loans and closing costs the VA limits', '<p>The VA caps certain closing costs lenders can charge veterans, and some fees common on conventional loans (like real estate attorney fees in some circumstances) cannot be charged to the veteran at all under VA rules. Sellers are also permitted to pay up to 4% of the loan amount toward the buyer\'s closing costs and prepaid items — a seller concession limit that doesn\'t exist in the same form on conventional loans.</p>' ],
        ],
        'terms'     => [ [ 'Funding fee', 'A one-time VA charge, a percentage of the loan, that can be financed.' ], [ 'Entitlement', 'The amount the VA will guarantee for your loan.' ], [ 'Certificate of Eligibility', 'The document that proves you qualify for a VA loan.' ], [ 'Seller concession', 'Money a seller agrees to contribute toward the buyer\'s closing costs, capped at 4% of the loan amount on VA loans.' ] ],
        'faqs'      => [
            [ 'Is there a down payment on a VA loan?', 'Not required for eligible borrowers with full entitlement, although a down payment lowers the funding fee.' ],
            [ 'Does a VA loan have PMI?', 'No. VA loans do not have monthly mortgage insurance.' ],
            [ 'Can I finance the funding fee?', 'Yes, it can be rolled into the loan, as in the example.' ],
            [ 'Who is exempt from the funding fee?', 'Veterans receiving VA disability compensation, Purple Heart recipients and some surviving spouses, among others. Check the VA’s list.' ],
            [ 'Is there a maximum VA loan amount?', 'For borrowers with full entitlement, the VA sets no maximum loan amount itself — your lender determines how much you qualify for based on income and debt-to-income ratio. Borrowers with reduced entitlement may face county-specific limits.' ],
            [ 'Can I use my VA loan benefit more than once?', 'Yes. The benefit can be reused for future home purchases, though a subsequent use typically carries a higher funding fee than a first-time use at the same down payment level.' ],
        ],
        'sources'   => [ [ 'U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs: Home loans', 'https://www.va.gov/housing-assistance/home-loans/' ], [ 'VA: Funding fee and closing costs', 'https://www.va.gov/housing-assistance/home-loans/funding-fee-and-closing-costs/' ] ],
        'related'   => [ 'va-loan-funding-fee-calculator', 'mortgage-calculator', 'fha-loan-calculator', 'refinance-calculator', 'amortization-calculator', 'home-equity-loan-calculator' ],
    ],

    'va-loan-funding-fee-calculator' => [
        'focus'     => 'va funding fee calculator',
        'secondary' => [ 'va loan funding fee', 'va funding fee 2026', 'va funding fee exemption', 'va irrrl funding fee' ],
        'title'     => 'VA Funding Fee Calculator (2026 Rates)',
        'meta'      => 'Free VA funding fee calculator: see your fee for purchase or refinance by down payment and first or subsequent use, plus who is exempt, and the payment impact.',
        'excerpt'   => 'Find your VA funding fee by down payment and use, see who is exempt, and see how the fee changes your payment.',
        'answer'    => 'The VA funding fee is a one-time charge on the loan. For a first-time purchase it is 2.15% with no down payment, 1.5% with 5% or more down and 1.25% with 10% or more down; subsequent use with no down payment is 3.3%, and an IRRRL is 0.5%. On a $400,000 loan at 2.15% the fee is $8,600.',
        'steps'     => [ 'Enter the home price and your down payment percentage.', 'Select purchase, refinance or IRRRL.', 'Choose your military category and whether this is your first use of the benefit.', 'Mark whether you receive VA disability compensation, which can exempt you, and read the fee and the monthly payment.' ],
        'formula'   => '<p><strong>Funding fee = loan amount × fee rate</strong>, where the rate depends on the loan type, down payment and whether you have used the benefit before. If you finance the fee, <strong>total loan = loan amount + fee</strong>.</p>',
        'example'   => '<p>First-use purchase rates (regular military):</p><ul><li>0% down: <strong>2.15%</strong> → on $400,000, <strong>$8,600</strong></li><li>5% to under 10% down: <strong>1.5%</strong></li><li>10% or more down: <strong>1.25%</strong></li><li>Subsequent use, 0% down: <strong>3.3%</strong>; IRRRL: <strong>0.5%</strong></li></ul><p>Financing $8,600 at 6.5% for 30 years adds about <strong>$54.36</strong> a month to the payment.</p>',
        'sections'  => [
            [ 'Who pays no funding fee', '<p>Veterans receiving VA compensation for a service-connected disability, Purple Heart recipients and some surviving spouses are exempt. Confirm your status on your Certificate of Eligibility or with your lender.</p>' ],
            [ 'How to lower the fee', '<p>A down payment of 5% or 10% lowers the rate. Whether it makes sense depends on how much cash you have and the interest rate. Test both in the calculator.</p>' ],
            [ 'Always confirm with the VA', '<p>Fee rates are set by the VA and can change. Check the current table on va.gov before you close. This tool is an estimate for planning.</p>' ],
            [ 'Purchase fee vs. refinance fee: they\'re not the same', '<p>The funding fee table for a regular purchase loan is different from the table for a cash-out refinance, which is again different from the IRRRL (streamline refinance) rate. A cash-out refinance funding fee runs noticeably higher than a standard purchase at the same down payment tier, since the VA treats pulling equity out as a different risk profile than financing a new purchase.</p>' ],
            [ 'Should you pay the fee upfront or finance it?', '<p>Financing the funding fee into the loan avoids an upfront cash hit but means paying interest on that fee for the full loan term, which adds up over 30 years. Paying it upfront in cash costs less in total but requires having that cash available at closing. Run both scenarios in this calculator to see the actual dollar trade-off for your specific loan amount and rate.</p>' ],
            [ 'Funding fee exemption: how to actually confirm it', '<p>Simply believing you qualify for a disability exemption isn\'t enough at closing — your lender needs documented confirmation of your VA disability compensation status, and processing that documentation can take time. If you believe you may be exempt, raise it with your lender as early as possible in the loan process, not right before closing, to avoid delays or having to pay the fee and seek a refund afterward.</p>' ],
        ],
